 a>&gt\;<br><b>Sent:</b> Thursday\, April 23\, 2026 5:03 PM<br><b>To:</b> <a
  href="mailto:admin@securitydepot.ca" target="_blank">admin@securitydepot.c
 a</a><br><b>Subject:</b> Argyle -Rooftop Door </p><p>The rooftop door (door
  leading onto roof) isn’t latching properly and blowing open in the wind.</
 p><p>Can you please send someone out ASAP to fix the door.</p><p>3932 Unive
 rsity Ave NW</p><p>-----------------------------</p><p><b>From:</b> directo
 r@securitydepot.ca&lt\;director@securitydepot.ca&gt\; <br><b>Sent:</b> Frid
 ay\, April 24\, 2026 11:45 AM<br><b>To:</b> Tristan Johnston &lt\;tjohnston
 @bluejeanmanagement.com&gt\;<br><b>Cc:</b> Darrell Security Depot &lt\;darr
 ell@securitydepot.ca&gt\;<br><b>Subject:</b> RE: Service Request | Argyle -
  Rooftop Door | J23740</p><p> G’Day Tristan\,</p><p>We did not get the mess
 age yesterday.  The doorhas blown off its hinges and needs a fair amount of
  work to repair.  Weare not in a position to do that today unfortunately wi
 th staff away andengaged.</p><p>It will need a new continuous hinge\, a new
  doorcloser\, some weather stripping and a more robust fixing point on the 
 non-activedoor for the push bar on the active door.  The anti-pry plate fit
 tedshould be removed in favor on weather stripping between the doors.  It i
 spossible that there was movement in the door and the wind got under the an
 t1pry plate and pulled it open.   Removing it means that the wind hasnowher
 e to apply leverage.  Unlikely spider man will climb up the buildingto pry 
 this door open to break in.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p>
